    spec_imports_declared_nowhere_2026-08-04, 61, "2026-11-10 p2-specimports lane (Fable): closed-obsolete. Premise 'unresolved braced-import names are resolved silently / never checked' no longer holds: the loader reports every unprovided braced name at load time (seed oracle warn_unprovided_use_names 98e468602fd; pure-Simple parity 2026-09-19 in module_loader_core.spl record_module_surface/warn_unprovided_use_names, unit-pinned by test/01_unit/compiler/interpreter/use_warning_unprovided_names_spec.spl 6/6). Re-measured on the deployed bin/simple.exe at b364745fdbf: fixture `use std.crypto.chacha20.{chacha20_block, chacha20_keystream}` (the record's own sharpest case; chacha20_keystream still declared nowhere) prints `[use-warning] 'chacha20_keystream' is named in use std.crypto.chacha20.{...} but module '.../chacha20.spl' does not provide it` and runs rc 0; a provided name prints nothing. Pinned end-to-end by new test/01_unit/compiler/interpreter/dead_braced_import_reported_spec.spl (2/2 green; this is a premise-check spec, not a red->green fix). Fail-closed was NOT implemented: it is a recorded design rejection in the seed, the pure loader and the pinning spec (mass-break, no migration path), and is concretely unsafe today - every `simple test` run already emits two real use-warnings from the stdlib test runner itself (build_coverage_wrapper_checked, discover_sdoctest_files_for_path_run: `use std.test_runner.*` from nogc_sync_mut files resolves family-first to the stale 18-line nogc_async_mut copies, which lack them), so a fatal oracle would break the runner before any spec runs. What remains is spec-tree CONTENT (1,003 dead names) plus seed oracle coverage gaps (no warning for a family-qualified path `std.nogc_sync_mut.io.process_ops.{eprint, bogus}` nor for a local re-export chain `export use a.{x}` -> `use b.{x, bogus}`), neither a module_loader_resolve.spl defect; both reported to the lane owner for separate shards."

